Open, and deliberately so.


Choosing a session server by what it has free
---------------------------------------------

A session should land on the application server that has the resources for
it, and today nothing measures that. HAProxy distributes the RDP entry
point by connection count, which is not the same thing: ten idle sessions
and ten compiling ones weigh alike.

LTSP 5 had an answer of sorts, and it is instructive that it never worked.
ldminfod listened on port 9571 and reported a figure computed from RAM,
swap and load; the greeter collected it from every server in LDM_SERVER
and then never sorted by it. By the time Debian dropped Python 2 the daemon
could not start at all, and nobody noticed - which says how much the
ranking had been doing. It was removed in ldm 3.4.0-1.

Whatever replaces it belongs in front of the session servers rather than in
the greeter. A client that must reach a second service to learn which
service to reach is the shape that already failed once, silently. Feeding
HAProxy real load data - an agent-check on each application server - keeps
the decision where the sessions are.


Done, kept here because the reasoning still applies
---------------------------------------------------

The list this file used to hold was the upstream one from 2007: SCREEN_xx
respawning, server autoconfiguration for dhcp3-server, installer hooks
choosing between ltsp-server and ltsp-server-standalone, NBD swap, and
bandwidth ideas like throttling the screensaver. None of it describes this
project. Respawning screens, DHCP and NBD configuration all work and are
documented in lts.conf(5) and ltsp-config(8); the packages named no longer
exist.
